{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310555,"nonce":"T0kJnHkprjaKeJSKJhD3EX4Ya3I=","title":"cleanup_all_scopes_except_symbol at explicit Return freed all non-returned heap values without capture analysis, leaving ...","message":"`hir_to_mir.rs::cleanup_all_scopes_except_symbol` (called at every explicit `Return`) used to emit `free` for every scoped heap value other than the returned variable. It had no notion of capture: if a local heap value flowed into another allocation's constructor and the outer allocation was the one being returned, the inner value was freed anyway, leaving the returned object holding a dangling pointer.\n\n```haxe\nstatic function build(reader:Reader):BPETokenizer {\n  var vocab = new Vocab();\n  for (t in tokens) vocab.add(t);\n  var tok = new BPETokenizer(vocab, byteLevel);  // captures `vocab`\n  return tok;\n}\n// caller's tok.vocabSize() -\u003e loads this.vocab (freed) -\u003e SIGSEGV\n```\n\nFixed in `64d98ea` by aligning the explicit-Return path with the already-correct implicit-end path (which calls `cleanup_drop_classes_only`): emit drop+free only for `@:derive(Drop)` classes, defer non-Drop allocations to `InsertFreePass` ([insert_free.rs:374](compiler/src/ir/insert_free.rs#L374)), which correctly sees a value passed as a constructor arg as escaping via that call and won't free it.\n\n**Why:** `InsertFreePass` has proper escape analysis; the HIR-level cleanup did not, but was duplicating its job. The right division of labour is: HIR-level cleanup runs eager drop semantics for user-controlled `@:derive(Drop)` types; non-Drop GC-style heap is the optimizer's responsibility.\n\n**How to apply:**\n- If you see a use-after-free where the freed value is held by a returned object's field, look at `cleanup_all_scopes_except_symbol`. It should NOT be freeing non-Drop allocations any more.\n- `exit_drop_scope` (block/loop scope exit) is left as-is: those values are guaranteed not to outlive the block, so unconditional free is safe — and removing it would leak loop-local allocs that `InsertFreePass` skips for inner-block allocs.\n- Related: [[bugs-interface-dispatch-elision]] is the next remaining bug blocking llama-smoke past `GGUFTokenizer.build`.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_cleanup_scopes_frees_captures` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}